From: Marionette AirDrop driver research
Subject: [EXPERIMENTAL] skip HAL SRNG teardown when HIF is already closed

Static audit of the 0031 Oops (target_if_deinit_dbr_ring+0x1f4, ESR
0x96000047, WnR=1) shows the faulting call chain is a normal driver
shutdown, not a module-unregister race:

  cnss_recovery_work_handler -> cnss_bus_dev_shutdown ->
  cnss_pci_dev_shutdown -> pld_pcie_shutdown -> wlan_hdd_pld_shutdown ->
  hdd_wlan_shutdown -> hdd_wlan_stop_modules -> hdd_check_for_leaks ->
  wlan_objmgr_vdev_release_ref -> wlan_objmgr_vdev_obj_free ->
  wlan_objmgr_pdev_vdev_detach -> wlan_objmgr_pdev_release_ref ->
  target_if_direct_buf_rx_pdev_destroy_handler ->
  target_if_deinit_dbr_ring

hdd_wlan_stop_modules() calls hdd_hif_close() at wlan_hdd_main.c:14922 and
only later hdd_check_for_leaks() at :14956. hdd_hif_close() clears the
QDF_MODULE_ID_HIF context (hdd_deinit_cds_hif_context, wlan_hdd_driver_ops.c)
and then hif_close() frees the HAL SRNG table. A monitor vdev whose delete
did not complete survives until hdd_check_for_leaks(), which force-releases
its reference and destroys the monitor PDEV after HAL is gone. DBR then
calls hal_srng_cleanup() with the stale hal_soc/srng pair.

This is why 0028-0031 only made things worse: 0028 deferred the WMA detach,
turning a warning-level delete into a 20 s timeout and leaving the vdev
leaked; the leaked vdev is what reaches the late destroy path.

The patch keeps host ring memory ownership unchanged and only skips the HAL
operation when the HIF context is already gone. Normal monitor delete
(HAL alive) is unchanged. It does not fix the STOP/DELETE ordering warning;
it makes shutdown survive it, which is required before any persistent use.

diff --git a/qca-wifi-host-cmn/target_if/direct_buf_rx/src/target_if_direct_buf_rx_main.c b/qca-wifi-host-cmn/target_if/direct_buf_rx/src/target_if_direct_buf_rx_main.c
--- a/qca-wifi-host-cmn/target_if/direct_buf_rx/src/target_if_direct_buf_rx_main.c
+++ b/qca-wifi-host-cmn/target_if/direct_buf_rx/src/target_if_direct_buf_rx_main.c
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 #include "hal_api.h"
 #include <service_ready_util.h>
 #include <init_deinit_lmac.h>
+#include <cds_api.h>
 
 /**
  * struct module_name : Module name information structure
@@ -2291,7 +2292,22 @@ static QDF_STATUS target_if_dbr_deinit_ring(struct wlan_objmgr_pdev *pdev,
 	dbr_ring_cfg = mod_param->dbr_ring_cfg;
 	if (dbr_ring_cfg) {
 		target_if_dbr_empty_ring(pdev, dbr_psoc_obj, mod_param);
-		hal_srng_cleanup(dbr_psoc_obj->hal_soc, dbr_ring_cfg->srng);
+		/*
+		 * hdd_hif_close() releases the HIF/HAL context before
+		 * hdd_check_for_leaks() force-drops vdev references that
+		 * survived a failed delete.  A leaked monitor vdev therefore
+		 * reaches this PDEV destroy callback with a stale hal_soc and
+		 * a stale SRNG pointer.  Host ring memory is still ours and
+		 * must be released, but touching HAL here writes through a
+		 * freed SRNG table.
+		 */
+		if (cds_get_context(QDF_MODULE_ID_HIF))
+			hal_srng_cleanup(dbr_psoc_obj->hal_soc,
+					 dbr_ring_cfg->srng);
+		else
+			direct_buf_rx_err("HAL already closed; skip srng cleanup for module %d srng %d",
+					  mod_param->mod_id,
+					  mod_param->srng_id);
 		qdf_mem_free_consistent(dbr_psoc_obj->osdev,
 					dbr_psoc_obj->osdev->dev,
 					dbr_ring_cfg->ring_alloc_size,
